---
Read and change the repository the project is in, with no git program: its place, history and status, and commits, branches, worktrees and merges under the effects ceiling, as JSON (ADR 0339)

Usage: specforge git [OPTIONS] <COMMAND>

Commands:
  repo         Where the repository is: its top directory, git directory and common directory, the current directory's prefix and the repository it is a submodule of
  head         HEAD's commit and branch, whether it is detached or unborn, and its upstream
  rev          What each revision names: its sha and kind, or that it names nothing
  status       The working tree's status: each changed, conflicted and untracked path, and whether it is clean
  config       Every value a configuration key has, and the one git applies
  log          The commits of a range, newest first, with their parents, people, message and trailers, and how many
  diff         The files that differ between a commit and another, the index or the worktree, with their line counts
  merge-base   The nearest commit two revisions share
  show         A file at a revision (`HEAD:path`): its blob, size and text
  branches     The local branches with their upstream and how far each is ahead and behind, or the remote-tracking ones
  worktrees    The main worktree and the linked ones, with the branch each is on
  remotes      The remotes, with each URL's host and `owner/repo` path
  submodules   The submodules `.gitmodules` declares: each name, path and URL
  add          Stage files as the worktree has them; ignore rules kept, a filter driver refused
  commit       Record a commit: stage the paths named, run the repository's hooks, commit by git's configured identity
  branch       Create or delete a branch
  switch       Move HEAD to a branch, its files checked out; a file the worktree changed is never overwritten
  tag          Create a tag: annotated with -m, else a plain ref
  worktree     Add, remove or prune linked worktrees
  merge        Merge a revision into HEAD: a fast-forward when history allows, else a merge commit; conflicts exit 1
  reset        Move HEAD to a revision, and the index (--mixed, the default) and the files (--hard) with it
  restore      Put files back as the index has them (or --source), discarding their changes; --staged: their index entries
  stash        Set the worktree's changes aside, bring them back, or list them
  cherry-pick  Apply a commit onto HEAD and record it with its author and message (or stage it, -n); conflicts exit 1
  revert       Record a commit that undoes another (or stage its undoing, -n); conflicts exit 1
  help         Print this message or the help of the given subcommand(s)

Options:
      --in <DIR>
          Run as if typed in this directory, as `git -C` runs: its project, the repository it is in, and every typed path read from it

  -h, --help
          Print help
